    "teacherReflection": "1. Which learners predicted that the colour would jump straight to blue, and did the dropwise data change their thinking?\n2. Did learners explain the antacid's action using H+ and OH- and the idea of salt + water, or only say that the acid 'disappears'?\n3. How well did groups control the dropwise addition, and what would you change so every group reaches the green endpoint safely?\n4. Which DQB questions need to be kept for Lessons 5 and 6, and did learners avoid mixing up neutralisation with fizzing?\n5. Was the 40 minutes balanced, roughly 5 minutes Predict, 15 Observe, 10 Explain, 4 DQB and 6 Model Building, and what would you adjust?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We added dilute sodium hydroxide to 10 cm3 of dilute hydrochloric acid with universal indicator and saw the colour change from red through orange and yellow to green after about 10 cm3 of base (illustrative result), and saw an antacid tablet raise the pH of dilute acid.",
      "learned": "Neutralisation is acid + base -> salt + water. Hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide gives sodium chloride + water, and hydrochloric acid + magnesium hydroxide gives magnesium chloride + water. The pH rises towards 7 when the acid is neutralised.",
      "explained": "Juma's heartburn is stomach acid pushed up into the food pipe. The antacid contains a base that neutralises the excess acid, forming a salt and water, so the burning stops. This explains the 'soothe' on Mama Amina's shelf."
    }
