    "overview": "Lesson 7 moves from the lab to the wider world. Learners recall salts they already met: sodium chloride in the tin, salts formed in neutralisation (Lesson 4) and washing soda that crumbles (Lesson 6). They predict where else salts matter, such as Mr. Otieno salting fish in Kisumu, Farmer Kiprop fertilising tea in Kericho, and clinics giving oral rehydration salts.\\n\\nIn groups of 4, learners research one application area (agriculture, food, medicine, laundry, road use) using digital devices or printed resource cards, and record uses and the salt involved. They then share findings in a jigsaw. In the Explain phase the class debates the motion: 'Fertiliser salts do more good than harm to Kenya.' They use evidence on eutrophication in Lake Victoria (nitrate and phosphate run-off, algae and water hyacinth, oxygen depletion, fish death), soil pollution and air pollution.\\n\\nThe lesson ends by adding new questions to the Driving Question Board and revising the Mombasa Kitchen Model: salts are useful (food, farming, medicine, laundry) but need proper use and the right amount. Excess salt in food and blood pressure is left for Lesson 8.",
